<article class="env-proposition" id="07QW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07QW">Proposition <span data-tag="07QW">15.53.3</span>.</a> The following types of rings are excellent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
fields, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Noetherian complete local rings, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathbf{Z}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Dedekind domains with fraction field of characteristic zero, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
finite type ring extensions of any of the above. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      See Propositions <a href="/tag/07PX" data-tag="07PX">15.51.12</a> and <a href="/tag/07PJ" data-tag="07PJ">15.49.7</a> to see that these rings are G-rings and have J-2. Any Cohen-Macaulay ring is universally catenary, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00NM" data-tag="00NM">10.105.9</a>. In particular fields, Dedekind rings, and more generally regular rings are universally catenary. Via the Cohen structure theorem we see that complete local rings are universally catenary, see Algebra, Remark <a href="/tag/032C" data-tag="032C">10.160.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>